          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have reflux and I also take ibuprofen for my knees.",
          "answer": "Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is a condition where stomach contents frequently move back into the esophagus, causing troublesome symptoms or damage to the esophageal lining. While some reflux is normal, it becomes a disease when it causes significant symptoms or injury. The usual cause is a temporary relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter, a muscle ring between the esophagus and stomach, sometimes made worse by a hiatal hernia (condition-gerd).\n\nIn the ICD-10-CM coding system, gastroesophageal reflux disease is classified as K21. This category distinguishes between reflux disease with esophagitis (inflammation of the esophagus) and without esophagitis (coding-gerd-k21).\n\nThe provided information does not detail the general effects of ibuprofen or provide specific advice on taking ibuprofen when you have reflux. If you have concerns about taking ibuprofen with reflux, it is best to discuss them with a healthcare provider.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
